---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import DocLayout from '~/layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { ui, localizedPath } from '~/i18n';
import { PROVIDER_COUNT } from '~/data/site';

const t = ui.en;
const entries = (await getCollection('deploy'))
  .filter((e) => e.id.startsWith('en/'))
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<DocLayout lang="en" path="/deploy" title={t.deployTitle} description={t.deployIntro}>
  <h1>{t.deployTitle}</h1>
  <p class="lead">{t.deployIntro}</p>

  <h2>How a certificate reaches a platform with CertMate</h2>
  <p>
    Issuance and delivery are separate steps. CertMate obtains the certificate
    from the CA over ACME, usually proving control of the domain with a DNS-01
    challenge through the API of your DNS provider ({PROVIDER_COUNT} are
    supported), and renews it 30 days before it expires. Getting the files onto
    the machine or cluster that serves them is then done in one of three ways.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pull it with the download URL.</strong>
      <code>GET /api/certificates/&#123;domain&#125;/download</code> returns a ZIP
      with <code>cert.pem</code>, <code>chain.pem</code>, <code>fullchain.pem</code>
      and <code>privkey.pem</code>;
      <code>/download/&#123;cert|chain|fullchain|privkey|combined&#125;</code>
      returns a single file. A viewer API key can fetch the public material
      only; <code>privkey</code> and <code>combined</code> need the operator
      role. This suits a cron job or a configuration-management run on the host
      that terminates TLS. See the <a href="/docs/api-reference.html">API reference</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write it into a Kubernetes Secret.</strong> The
      <code>kubernetes-secret</code> typed deploy target applies the full chain
      and key to a <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> Secret with Server-Side Apply,
      on issuance and on every renewal. It authenticates with an API server URL
      and bearer token, or with the service account when CertMate runs in the
      cluster; no <code>kubectl</code> is involved.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run a deploy hook.</strong> A shell command, run with
      <code>sh -c</code> after a certificate is created or renewed, with the
      domain and file paths in <code>CERTMATE_*</code> environment variables.
      It runs inside the CertMate container, which has <code>sh</code>,
      <code>bash</code>, <code>curl</code> and <code>openssl</code> but not
      <code>docker</code>, <code>kubectl</code> or <code>ssh</code>, so a hook
      reaches other systems over HTTP. See <a href="/docs/deploy-hooks.html">Deploy hooks</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A hook or target that fails is logged, audited and raises the
    <code>deploy_hook_failed</code> alert; it does not block the certificate
    operation. The guides below cover the platform side: where each one reads
    its certificate and how to make it pick up a renewed one.
  </p>

  <h2>Guides</h2>

  <ul class="not-prose mt-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {entries.map((e) => (
      <li>
        <a
          href={localizedPath('en', `/deploy/${e.id.replace(/^en\//, '')}`)}
          class="flex h-full items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-4 transition-colors hover:border-primary dark:border-gray-700"
        >
          <Icon name={e.data.icon} class="mt-0.5 text-xl text-primary" />
          <span>
            <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{e.data.platform}</span>
            <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">{e.data.summary}</span>
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
